      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,-10,\\,-14⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨4,\\,-10,\\,-14⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,\\,10,\\,14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the scalar multiplication rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-4,\\,10,\\,14⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,5,\\,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
